Setting. This is a working sheet from the 'Teichmülleries' (1981–82). It treats the simplest piece of the Teichmüller tower: a torus, possibly with a hole, obtained by gluing two cylinders. Grothendieck's surfaces come with oriented boundary data, and he looks at the extended mapping class group, which includes orientation-reversing maps. Here that group is GL(2,Z) acting on H_1.

The gluing. In the drawing, a handle-cylinder X' (an arc-shaped tube) is glued onto a horizontal cylinder X''. Each cylinder X has two ends ε_0(X) and ε_1(X), the boundary circles a_0 and a_1. The bracket on the page says:
- identifications ε_0(X') ≅ ε_1(X'') and ε_1(X') ≅ ε_0(X'') glue the two pieces into a torus X = X' ∪ X'';
- these identifications induce an identification ω(X') ≅ ω(X'') of the orientation torsors (or orientation modules) ω.

Next to this is the remark 'anticommutatif', with a circled minus and an arrow to ω(X'∧X''). This records that the natural pairing ω(X')⊗ω(X'')→ω(X'∧X'') is antisymmetric. Read in modern terms: the orientation of the glued torus, equivalently the sign of the intersection form on H_1, depends on the order in which the two cylinders are taken. Exchanging X' and X'' introduces a sign.

Involutions and twists. σ' and σ'' are orientation-reversing involutions (reflections) of the configuration, one adapted to each cylinder. The text says they 'do not depend on the choice of orientation(s)'. The core curves e' and e'' (in the drawing, vectors at the junction) fix the orientations of the two pieces. They also fix a '2-orientation', i.e. an orientation of the surface. This determines the Dehn twists ρ' (along e') and ρ'' (along e'').

On H_1 = Z e' ⊕ Z e'' the formulas are:
- σ' = diag(1, −1) and σ'' = diag(−1, 1);
- ρ'(e') = e' and ρ'(e'') = e' + e'', a transvection;
- σ swaps e' and e''.

The relations recorded are:
- σ'ρ'σ' = σ''ρ'σ'' = ρ'^{-1};
- the same for ρ'';
- σρ'σ = ρ'', σρ''σ = ρ'.

These are the standard facts that an orientation-reversing element inverts a Dehn twist, and that the swap exchanges the two twists. Together they give the presentation of the extended (1,1) modular group GL(2,Z), or PGL(2,Z) after dividing by ±1, as generated by reflections and twists. This is the kind of data Grothendieck needs for the 'Lego' presentation of the Teichmüller tower in its first non-trivial genus-1 level.

The dashed prism at top left is not explained on the page. Plausibly it is a cell picture of the glued surface or of a relation (a polyhedron of moves). It could not be decoded with confidence.
